```
=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router1
diagnosis_text:
The fault is the complete absence of the bgpd process on router1. The FRR
service on router1 is in an "inactive (dead)" state, meaning bgpd was killed
or crashed and was never restarted. Without bgpd, router1 cannot open a BGP
TCP session on port 179 toward router2 (193.10.11.2). Router2's BGP daemon
(AS 2, router-id 200.1.1.1) is healthy and keeps retrying the connection to
193.10.11.1, but because nothing is listening on that address, the session
never leaves the Active state and no prefixes are exchanged in either
direction.

As a consequence, router1's RIB contains only its two directly connected
prefixes (193.10.11.0/24 and 195.11.14.0/24) — there is no route to
200.1.1.0/24, so any packet from pc1 (195.11.14.2) destined for pc2
(200.1.1.2) is silently black-holed at router1. Symmetrically, router2 never
learns 195.11.14.0/24, so return traffic would also fail. This fully explains
why pc1 can reach its own gateway (router1's eth1, 195.11.14.1 — reachable
via a connected route) but cannot reach anything beyond it in AS 2.

The valid BGP configuration already exists on disk in router1's /etc/frr/frr.conf
(router bgp 1, neighbor 193.10.11.2 remote-as 2, network 195.11.14.0/24, no
bgp ebgp-requires-policy), so no configuration repair is needed.

Root cause: bgpd was killed on router1 and the FRR service is dead.

Corrective fix: Run `systemctl start frr` on router1. This will restart bgpd,
which will load frr.conf, establish the eBGP session with router2, and
exchange the two prefixes (195.11.14.0/24 ↔ 200.1.1.0/24), fully restoring
pc1-to-pc2 connectivity.
=== END ===
